<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The Mac app's sandbox: nothing but what it needs. The identifiers are build settings from
  ../../Config/TwoNote.xcconfig: TWONOTE_MAC_BUNDLE_ID is <prefix>.mac, TeamIdentifierPrefix and
  AppIdentifierPrefix are "<team>.". This file is used when a team is set (Local.xcconfig);
  without one the app is signed ad hoc with TwoNoteMac-NoTeam.entitlements instead.
  - app-sandbox: the app sees only its own container (~/Library/Containers/<prefix>.mac),
    where the encrypted store lives, and the App Group container below.
  - network.client: outgoing connections only, to the user's own TwoNote server (HTTP and the
    WebSocket for live updates). No server role, no incoming connections.
  - application-groups: the folder the app shares with its share extension and its widget
    (~/Library/Group Containers/<team>.<prefix>.mac). What may be in it is listed in
    ../../TwoNote/Shared/SharedContainer.swift: a public key, sealed items, and - only if the
    user turns it on - page titles for the widget.
    The group is team-prefixed, the Mac's own form: macOS ties a team-prefixed group to the
    signing team itself, so no App Group has to be registered in the developer account and
    the system never asks the user whether TwoNote may "access data from other apps" (which
    it does for a group. identifier that the provisioning profile does not authorize).
  No file access, no camera, no microphone, no Apple events.

  - keychain-access-groups: the data-protection Keychain, as on the iPhone (the Secret Key, the
    session token, and the Touch ID item). That Keychain needs an application identifier,
    which macOS accepts only from a provisioning profile; this entry (Xcode's "Keychain
    Sharing", with the app's own identifier as its only group, so nothing is shared) is what
    makes automatic signing fetch the development profile for <prefix>.mac, which then adds
    com.apple.application-identifier and com.apple.developer.team-identifier to the signature.
    Without an entitlement that needs a profile, Xcode signs a Mac app without one.
  The extensions need no Keychain, hence no profile.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)$(TWONOTE_MAC_BUNDLE_ID)</string>
	</array>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(TWONOTE_MAC_BUNDLE_ID)</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
